		heading: 'What “hallowed” means',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Hallowed</em> means <strong>treated as holy &mdash; set apart, honoured, held sacred</strong>. It comes from the same old English root as <em>holy</em>, and it is a verb, not a decoration: to hallow something is to regard it as set apart rather than ordinary.</p><p>So &ldquo;hallowed be thy name&rdquo; is not a compliment paid to God before the requests begin. It is the first petition of the prayer &mdash; a request that God&rsquo;s name be treated as holy. The Greek verb sits in a form that asks for something to happen, which is why modern translations often render it &ldquo;may your name be honoured&rdquo; or &ldquo;let your name be kept holy.&rdquo;</p><p>Asked plainly: <em>let who you are be known and honoured as what it is.</em> That is the opening ask of the prayer Jesus taught.</p>`,
