		bodyHtml: `<p>Most people know the line as &ldquo;hallowed be thy name&rdquo; because that is the King James wording, carried into centuries of liturgy and memory. <em>Thy</em> is simply the older English possessive &mdash; the equivalent of <em>your</em>. Nothing in the meaning turns on it.</p><p>What is worth knowing is that <em>thou</em> and <em>thy</em> were the <strong>familiar, intimate</strong> forms in older English, the words used with family and close friends, while <em>you</em> was the formal address for superiors and strangers. So the archaic phrasing that now sounds most distant to us was, in its own day, the most personal wording available.</p><p>The Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible uses &ldquo;your name&rdquo; because that is what a reader today hears as ordinary and close &mdash; the same instinct the original phrasing was serving.</p>`,
